at makes borrowing more expensive, but the market is still functioning. Norvane Utilities priced a C$1.1B bond \"comfortably\" in Q3.\n- **Norvane:** It is rated stable and has shown it can issue. It looks able to finance its 2027 plan through debt, at wider spreads than earlier in 2026.\n- **Pellham Power:** It is the largest buyer of grid equipment in Caldria. Its C$2.4B equity offering was pulled on 14 October. Caldria Ratings then put it on negative watch. A downgrade would raise its debt costs and could limit how much it can borrow. Pellham has said it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and resize its Q1 2027 tender. That suggests it can't fully fund the original plan.\n- **Equity is the weak channel:** Two utility share offerings were cut back in Q3, and Pellham's was pulled. That leaves debt as the main route, with more pressure on credit metrics.\n\n**Caveats:**\n- I didn't open the utility tender calendar or Pellham's market statement, so I haven't checked the 2027 capex amounts or Pellham's funding gap.\n- I found no data on debt maturities or leverage.\n- The Norvane evidence is a single bond deal.\n\n**What this means for grid-equipment demand:** Pellham's 40% cut in transformer orders is the main downside. Norvane's plan looks fundable.","ttft_ms":4463,"type":"result","duration_ms":34272,"uuid":"97bf4e55-d86e-4ae5-bae4-7a3b1ada1885","ttft_stream_ms":4219,"time_to_request_ms":134,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":12,"system_prompt":7,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":7,"input_attachments":22,"input_hooks":6,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":4,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":16,"init_frame_send":4,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":5,"message_prep":0,"model_config_wait":4,"autocompact":5,"request_announcements":15,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build":6,"message_normalization":4,"client_creation":6,"request_build":9},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":81,"time_to